          "kind": "finished",
          "message": "The implementation is complete and validated. Summary of what I built and verified:\n\n## Implementation (root `ini` package, one file per concern)\n\n| File | Role |\n| --- | --- |\n| `parser.mbt` | `parse`: single pass over physical lines — blank/comment skip, header opens/re-opens a section, entries go to the current map |\n| `entry.mbt` | one key/value line: first `=`/`:` as separator, key trim + validation |\n| `value.mbt` | carriers (`Unquoted`/`DoubleQuoted`/`SingleQuoted`), per-carrier escape table, quoted scanning, inline comments, backslash continuations |\n| `section.mbt` | `[name]` headers (trim, fully-quoted name loses one pair, nested/unclosed/empty rejected), key validation |\n| `printer.mbt` | `print`: globals then sections, every value as a double-quoted carrier (`\\\\ \\\" \\n \\r \\t`), extra quote pair for quote-wrapped section names |\n| `text.mbt` | char classes, LF/CRLF line splitting, trimming, comment scanning, trailing-backslash counting |\n| `error.mbt` | `pub suberror IniParseError` with `line N: reason` messages |\n\nKey rules implemented: code-point scanning (unicode/emoji safe), first separator wins (`url=http://h:8080/p?a=b`), quoted values keep blanks/`;`/`#` and close only on their own line with only blanks/comment after, per-carrier escapes (`'a\\nb'` keeps its backslash), inline comments only after a blank and stripped *before* the marker check (so a `\\` inside a comment can't swallow the next line), odd/even trailing-backslash runs decided on the raw line before decoding, marker at EOF rejected, continuation lines contribute text not syntax, duplicate keys last-wins, section re-open.\n\n## Tests and checks actually run\n\n- `moon test` → **124/124 pass**: 12 public (incl. both property tests) + 112 added, derived from `specs/ini.md` (entries, sections, values/escapes/comments/continuations, invalid inputs, print round-trip, real-world git-config/systemd/php.ini/CRLF) plus a deterministic sweep over every ≤3-char string from the grammar's significant alphabet and a two-entry piece sweep (each input must be rejected or round-trip; no panics).\n- `moon check --output-json` → **0 errors, 3 warnings**, all three pre-existing in files I must not edit (`ini_pub_test.mbt`, `ini_spec.mbt`, `moon.pkg`). No diagnostics originate in the new code.\n- `moon fmt --check` → every added file is formatted; the only reported file is the provided `ini_spec.mbt`, which I restored byte-for-byte after `moon fmt` rewrote its `derive(...)` spelling.\n- `moon info` regenerated `pkg.generated.mbti` (now lists `pub suberror IniParseError`); the command was then blocked by the sandbox allowlist on a later step, so the file is current but the tool cannot be re-run here.\n- An independent explore-subagent audit compared code against `specs/ini.md`; its one substantive finding (backslash inside an inline comment still continuing the value) is fixed, and the remaining points are documented judgment calls.\n\n## Caveats\n- Judgment calls, documented in `README.md`: a trailing comment after `[name]` is accepted; continuation lines are appended raw (their leading blanks kept); \"blank\" means space/tab only; `print` cannot represent hand-built keys such as `#a`, `;a`, `[a` or names holding brackets (no text denotes them; `parse` never produces them).\n- The withheld private suite cannot be run here; `moon test` exercises only the public cases plus mine."
